Pin the ext4 directory hash seed

mke2fs picks a random hash seed when it is not given one. It changes
nothing a user can see and it changes the bytes of the filesystem, which
makes it the worst kind of reproducibility defect: invisible in a
file-level diff of the root filesystem, so two trees compare equal while
the images differ.

The filesystem UUID is already pinned here, via SYSTEM_UUID. The hash
seed is the one that was missed.

Appended to the existing mkfs arguments rather than replacing them, and
only when the variable is set, so a build that has not been told about
this behaves exactly as before.

--- a/image/gpt/ab_userdata/pre-image.sh
+++ b/image/gpt/ab_userdata/pre-image.sh
@@
-MKE2FS_ARGS_SYSTEM="-U $SYSTEM_UUID ${IGconf_fs_ext4_mkfs_args:-}"
+# -E hash_seed=, which mke2fs otherwise picks at random. It changes nothing a
+# user can see and it changes the bytes, so it is invisible in a diff of the
+# root filesystem while making two images differ.
+MKE2FS_HASH_SEED=""
+if [ -n "${NULLROUTE_EXT4_HASH_SEED:-}" ]; then
+	MKE2FS_HASH_SEED="-E hash_seed=${NULLROUTE_EXT4_HASH_SEED}"
+fi
+
+MKE2FS_ARGS_SYSTEM="-U $SYSTEM_UUID ${MKE2FS_HASH_SEED} ${IGconf_fs_ext4_mkfs_args:-}"
 EROFS_ARGS_SYSTEM="-U $SYSTEM_UUID ${IGconf_fs_erofs_mkfs_args:-}"
